# =============================================================================
# Stellereum testnet — shared genesis environment
# =============================================================================
#
# The node does NOT read this file itself. `src/main.rs` reads the process
# environment with `std::env::var`, so this file is a plain shell fragment you
# source before `stellereum run`:
#
#     set -a; . ./testnet/genesis.env; set +a
#     ./target/release/stellereum run --config testnet/node-a.yaml
#
# `set -a` is what exports the assignments. Without it these are shell
# variables and the node will not see them.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT MUST BE IDENTICAL ON EVERY NODE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two rules decide whether a set of nodes is one chain or several:
#
#   (1) STELL_FAUCET_KEY must be byte-for-byte identical on every node.
#       `src/main.rs` derives the faucet account from this key and credits
#       GENESIS_FAUCET_BALANCE to it during genesis. The faucet account is
#       therefore part of the genesis state, and the genesis state root is the
#       fingerprint of the chain. One node with a different key produces a
#       different account set, hence a different genesis state root, hence a
#       different chain. The nodes will each run happily, each finalize its own
#       blocks, and the chain splits silently. There is no handshake that
#       catches this.
#
#   (2) Every node must derive the SAME validator set. The set is built by
#       `build_validator_set` in src/main.rs as:
#           { this node's own Ed25519 key }
#           UNION
#           { the ed_pub of every entry in `peers:` whose `node-id` is listed
#             in `validators:` in the same YAML file }
#       So node-a.yaml and node-b.yaml must list the same node ids in
#       `validators:`, and each must carry a `peers:` entry with the matching
#       `ed-pub`. A node that forgets a peer in its own YAML ends up with a
#       smaller set, funds fewer accounts at genesis, and diverges on the same
#       fingerprint. `validators:` is node-id based (the `inf1:...` 20-byte
#       id), while the account that gets funded is the peer's `ed-pub` — the
#       two are not the same value, which is easy to get wrong.
#
# The node also refuses to start if the data directory it was given was created
# with different genesis parameters. It recomputes the genesis root from the
# current config and compares it to the one persisted under the `meta` key
# `genesis_state_root`. Mismatch is a hard `bail!` with both roots printed, not
# a warning. This is the safety net for (1) and (2), but it fires per data
# directory, so a node that has never run before will not catch your mistake —
# it will happily start a second chain.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT DOES NOT HAVE TO MATCH
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The following are per-node local policy and do not enter the state root:
#   STELL_RPC_LISTEN, STELL_EXPLORER_LISTEN, STELL_RATE_LIMIT_PER_SEC,
#   STELL_RPC_AUTH_TOKEN, STELL_ARCHIVE, STELL_KEEP_BLOCKS, STELL_MIN_BLOCK_MS
#
# STELL_MIN_GAS_LIMIT is per-node admission policy (it is a mempool base fee,
# not consensus state), but see the note below: running it differently across
# nodes gives you an uneven mempool, not a split chain. Set it the same anyway.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TESTNET FAUCET KEY — REPLACE THIS PLACEHOLDER
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 32 bytes of ed25519 seed, hex, no 0x prefix (64 hex characters).
#
# >>> THIS IS A PLACEHOLDER, NOT A KEY. IT MUST BE REPLACED BEFORE ANY NODE
# >>> IS STARTED, AND THE REPLACEMENT MUST BE SHARED VERBATIM WITH EVERY OTHER
# >>> NODE IN THE NETWORK. <<<
#
# The value below is `PLACEHOLDER_...` and is not valid hex, so the node will
# decode it to None and simply skip faucet funding — the chain will start
# happily with no faucet account, which is exactly the silent failure you do
# not want. A node started with an invalid key is indistinguishable, at
# genesis, from a node started with no key at all.
#
# Generate a real one with:
#     head -c 32 /dev/urandom | xxd -p -c 64
#
# Derive the account address it controls (this is the address you fund / query):
#     ./target/release/stellereum wallet address --from-key <the hex you generated>
#
# Two related settings, with deliberately different scopes:
#
#   STELL_FAUCET_KEY  -> set on EVERY node. It decides which account is credited
#                        at genesis, so it must match everywhere (rule 1 above).
#   STELL_FAUCET      -> set to 1 on AT MOST ONE node. It only decides whether
#                        that node serves the `stell_faucet` RPC method and the
#                        /api/faucet gateway route. Turning it on everywhere
#                        does not mint anything extra, but it does give you N
#                        independent faucet instances with N independent
#                        nonce counters all signing from the same account (see
#                        OPERATIONS.md).
#
# The faucet itself does not write balances. It signs an ordinary transfer from
# the pre-funded genesis account into the mempool, so the value movement is
# decided by consensus like any other transaction.
STELL_FAUCET_KEY=PLACEHOLDER_REPLACE_WITH_64_HEX_CHARS_0000000000000000000000000000000000000


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ENABLE THE FAUCET ENDPOINT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1 or true turns the faucet on for this node; anything else (including unset)
# leaves it off and `stell_faucet` answers -32601 "faucet is disabled on this
# node". Default: unset = disabled.
#
# Set this on ONE node only. The drip amount defaults to 10 STELL
# (`Faucet::default_drip_nano` in src/rpc.rs, overridable with
# STELL_FAUCET_DRIP_STELL) and the lifetime cap per address (1000 STELL) is
# hardcoded in src/main.rs where the Faucet is constructed; the cap is not
# configurable from the environment.
STELL_FAUCET=0


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FEE FLOOR / MINIMUM GAS LIMIT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Base fee for mempool admission, in nano-STELL. A transaction whose `gas_limit`
# is below this is rejected with MempoolError::FeeTooLow ("offered fee is below
# the base fee"), surfaced by `stell_sendRawTransaction` as JSON-RPC error
# -32000 with the message "rejected: ...".
#
# Why a floor is needed at all: with base_fee 0 a transaction with gas_limit 0
# is charged nothing, still occupies a block slot, and the proposer is paid
# exactly as much as for real work. That is a free-spam block.
#
# Default (when unset): svm::GAS_BASIC_TRANSFER = 280,000, the cost of the
# cheapest real transfer. 280,000 nano-STELL = 0.00028 STELL.
#
# This is admission policy, not consensus state — the value is not in the state
# root. Still set it the same on every node: a node with a lower floor will
# accept transactions its peers refuse to propagate.
STELL_MIN_GAS_LIMIT=280000


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BLOCK PACING FLOOR
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Minimum milliseconds between two blocks produced by this node. Local policy,
# not a consensus rule: a block's validity does not depend on when it was made,
# and every validator still accepts whatever arrives.
#
# It exists because a small validator set finalizes as fast as the CPU allows
# (measured ~5000 empty blocks/second on a single node), which floods the disk
# and the log. 400 ms caps a single node at ~2.5 blocks/second.
#
# Default: 400. Set 0 to remove the guard (the in-process simulator and the
# benchmarks rely on this).
STELL_MIN_BLOCK_MS=400


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RETENTION — SET THIS EXPLICITLY, DO NOT RELY ON THE DEFAULT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STELL_ARCHIVE is inverted: archive is ON unless the value is 0 or "false"
# (case-insensitive). Every other value, including an empty string, means
# archive. The default when the variable is unset is also archive.
#
# Archive keeps every block body forever. That is the only mode in which a
# historical block can be re-executed later for an audit; dropping a body is
# irreversible. The header record is written in BOTH modes, so the chain can
# always be walked.
#
# Set STELL_ARCHIVE=1 to be explicit about wanting the default, and pair it
# with a STELL_KEEP_BLOCKS value that is then ignored. Write it out anyway:
# a reader should not have to know which way round the flag is.
STELL_ARCHIVE=1

# Only consulted when STELL_ARCHIVE is 0/false. Block bodies older than
# `height - keep_blocks` are deleted; headers are kept. Default: 50000.
# Retention runs on the same cadence as the state snapshot job (every 100
# heights) and each pass removes at most PRUNE_BATCH (20000) bodies, because
# the background flusher is the only writer and an unbounded scan would stall
# every other write behind it.
STELL_KEEP_BLOCKS=50000


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LISTENERS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JSON-RPC. Default: 127.0.0.1:8545. For a node reachable from outside its
# host, bind 0.0.0.0 and put the auth token below in place. Note the router
# serves a single POST "/" route, so this is also where `stell_sendRawTransaction`
# lives.
STELL_RPC_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:8545

# Explorer HTML + read-only JSON gateway, Prometheus /metrics, and a nested
# copy of the JSON-RPC router at /rpc. Default: 127.0.0.1:8080.
STELL_EXPLORER_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:8080

# Per-IP fixed-window rate limit for the whole gateway surface, requests per
# second. Default: explorer::DEFAULT_RATE_LIMIT_PER_SEC = 30. Exceeding it
# returns HTTP 429 "rate limit exceeded" from every gateway route.
STELL_RATE_LIMIT_PER_SEC=30

# When set to a non-empty value, `stell_sendRawTransaction` requires
# params.token (or params.apiKey) to equal it, or it answers -32001
# "unauthorized". Unset or empty means writes are allowed with no
# authentication — fine on a laptop, not on a public endpoint.
#
# Note the scope precisely: the token gates `stell_sendRawTransaction` only.
# `stell_faucet` is a write method too — it signs a transfer from the faucet
# account into the mempool — and it is NOT gated by this token. Rate-limiting
# the gateway and putting it behind a reverse proxy is the only control on it.
STELL_RPC_AUTH_TOKEN=


# =============================================================================
# COMPILE-TIME GENESIS PARAMETERS — NOT ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
# =============================================================================
# Recorded here so an operator can verify the chain they are joining. None of
# the values below can be changed without rebuilding the binary, and every node
# MUST run the same binary: they are baked into the genesis state root, so two
# nodes built from different source revisions with different values here will
# refuse to share a data directory and will not agree on genesis.
#
#   GENESIS_VALIDATOR_BALANCE   100_000_000_000_000_000  (100,000,000 STELL)
#                               credited to EVERY account in the genesis
#                               validator set. Same constant for every node,
#                               applied to the same set, so the set of funded
#                               accounts is identical everywhere.
#
#   GENESIS_FAUCET_BALANCE      1_000_000_000_000_000      (1,000,000 STELL)
#                               credited to the account derived from
#                               STELL_FAUCET_KEY. Omitted entirely when that
#                               variable is unset or undecodable.
#
#   GENESIS_VALIDATOR_BOND      = staking::MIN_VALIDATOR_BOND = 1,000 STELL
#                               Each genesis validator is also bonded in the
#                               staking ledger, not merely funded. The
#                               validator set is re-derived from that ledger at
#                               every height boundary, so funding an account
#                               without bonding it would leave the set empty.
#                               An empty set has zero total power, and a
#                               zero-power set would let a single vote finalize
#                               every block; `quorum_power` now returns an
#                               unreachable power for that case so the chain
#                               stalls instead.
#
#   chain_id                    5757 — reported by `stell_chainId`. Hardcoded.
#
#   block reward                317_000_000 nano-STELL per block, paid to the
#                               proposer. Hardcoded at the runtime
#                               construction site.
#
#   faucet drip / cap           10 STELL per request (default; override with
#                               STELL_FAUCET_DRIP_STELL), 1000 STELL lifetime
#                               cap per recipient address. Cap hardcoded at the
#                               Faucet construction site.
#
#   max txs / block             10,000
#   max gas / block             120,000,000
#   state snapshot cadence      every 100 heights
#   recent blocks in RPC window 100
#   receipts retained in memory 50,000
#   consensus vote timeout      3 seconds, then view change
# =============================================================================
